gh treatment selection and follow-up, including integrating pathology and radiology information into staging and management decisions [Data: Entities (25); Relationships (5527, 8164, 8165, 10008, 14502, +more)].\\n\\nMultiple relationships reinforce this centrality by tying the DOCTOR to the act of discussing test results, using cancer test results for TNM scoring, and assigning those TNM component scores, indicating that much of the community’s information flow converges on clinician interpretation and patient-clinician discussion rather than standalone tools or institutions [Data: Entities (2945, 3900, 3901, 25); Relationships (5527, 8164, 8165)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Patient-to-clinician communication is framed around symptom escalation and allowable channels (calling and texting)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes explicit patient questions about when they should contact the clinician in response to symptoms or side effects, and whether texting is an acceptable communication method. These two communication modes suggest an emphasis on timely escalation and clarity about how to reach the clinical team [Data: Entities (6395, 6396); Relationships (14584, 14585)].\\n\\nAlthough the patient entity is not described in the provided entity list, the relationships show patient-initiated communication as a structured part of care navigation, indicating operational considerations (triage, response expectations, and communication policy) that can affect care continuity and patient safety [Data: Entities (6395, 6396); Relationships (14584, 14585)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Cancer test results are directly connected to TNM staging via scoring assigned by the DOCTOR\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Cancer test results are used by the DOCTOR as the basis for assigning TNM scores, and a SCORE entity is explicitly linked to the DOCTOR as the assigned letter-number rating within the TNM system. This captures a key workflow: diagnostic o