links indicate that fertility is treated both as a planning domain (a factor to weigh early) and as a risk communication obligation tied to treatment choices, which can influence consent, timing, and selection among options [Data: Entities (2774, 25); Relationships (5137, 10008)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Management of surgical menopause symptoms includes HRT and non-hormonal medication options, potentially involving a specialized symptom team\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The DOCTOR is linked to recommending HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY (HRT) as a management option if surgical menopause symptoms occur, and also to suggesting NON-HORMONAL MEDICINE when hormone therapy may not be suitable. This establishes a symptom-management branch in the community that complements cancer-directed treatment planning [Data: Entities (2227, 2226, 25); Relationships (3811, 3812)].\\n\\nA MENOPAUSAL SYMPTOM TEAM is connected to HRT decisions, indicating a consultative pathway to determine appropriateness of hormone therapy after surgery-induced menopause. This suggests multidisciplinary input for symptom management and risk/benefit evaluation [Data: Entities (2223, 2227); Relationships (3814)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The MRI technical subnetwork highlights both capabilities (staging protocols) and operational constraints (safety and patient experience)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"MRI is described as a radiation-free imaging modality that can be important for cancer evaluation (notably rectal cancer assessment), and it is explicitly connected to specialized MRI staging protocol techniques for accurate staging. It also relies on a computer imaging system, reflecting a defined technical workflow from signal acquisition to image generation [Data: Entities (424, 2706, 431); Relationships (583, 5018)].\\n\\nOperationally, multiple relationships identify constraints and patient-experience factors: devices such as PACEMAKER, certain HEART MONITOR types, and some SURGICAL CLIPS can make MRI unsafe or not allowed; straps may be used for positioning; small enclosed spaces can cause anxiety; an enema may be needed for rectal-area visualization; and warmth may be felt during the exam. Together these details characterize MRI as both high-value and procedurally complex [Data: Entities (2704, 2703, 2705, 2739, 4445, +more); Relationships (5015, 5014, 5017, 5020, 5024, +more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"CT imaging is presented as a complementary modality with distinct hardware dependencies and use in spread assessment\",\n      \"explanation\": \"CT scanning is described as an imaging procedure that uses an X-ray machine linked to a computer to generate cross-sectional images and 3-D reconstructions, supporting assessment of whether cancer has spread beyond an initial lesion. This establishes CT as a technically distinct alternative or complement to MRI, with specific dependencies on an X-ray machine and a computer imaging system [Data: Entities (423, 430, 431); Relationships (578, 579)].\\n\\nIn the overall community, CT’s explicit hardware relationships (X-RAY MACHINE and COMPUTER) contrast with MRI’s contraindication and comfort considerations, suggesting that modality selection can be influenced by both clinical question and feasibility/safety constraints [Data: Entities (423, 430, 431, 424); Relationships (578, 579, 583, 5015)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Clinical standards and clinician qualifications appear as trust and governance signals (NCCN guidelines and board certification)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The DOCTOR is linked to NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology via a prompt that patients ask whether the doctor consults these guidelines when considering treatment options. This introduces an external standard-of-care reference point within the community, implying that guideline alignment is treated as relevant to decision quality and transparency [Data: Entities (6341, 25); Relationships (